{
	title: 'Web tabs',
	description: `The web page shows the host's tabs (task 0a) and one of them. The address is /<sessionId>, so a tab can be linked and Back/Forward move between tabs: choosing a tab pushes a history entry, and the page landing on a tab it did not ask for (the tab closed, an unknown id) replaces it instead. With no valid id in the address, the page shows the tab it showed last (localStorage), else the first tab, else asks tab-start. The host serves the page at any path shaped like a session id; GET /session goes away.
A tab strip names each tab by number and name with a working dot, a question mark or an attention bell, and has a new tab button (in the shown tab's cwd) and a close button per tab; tabs are links (<a href>), so Cmd- or middle-click opens one in a browser tab. On narrow screens the strip becomes one button opening a sheet that lists the tabs with the same actions; the strip never scrolls sideways (it would fight the back-swipe gesture). Keys where the browser lets the page have them: Alt-1..9 and Alt-0, Ctrl-N/P and Ctrl-T/W on macOS (on other systems the browser keeps Ctrl-T/W/N; the buttons do it). Each tab keeps its own composer draft (already per session) and scroll position while another is shown.
Test the router and the key mapping as plain modules without a browser; check the strip and sheet in a real browser at phone and desktop widths.`,
